{"decision":"FIX","summary":"The additive representation is a plausible, general retrieval experiment, but descriptor classification is too permissive for a catalog-wide index rebuild and its tests do not demonstrate the stated retrieval separation robustly.","issues":["`\"регулиру\" in evidence` classifies negated text such as «нерегулируемый» or «не регулирующий» as `регулирующее исполнение`. This creates incorrect catalogue facts and can promote unsuitable products for regulating requests.","The ball-valve gate is substring-based (`\"кран\"` and `\"шаров\"` anywhere in name/type), so incidental text can receive execution descriptors. For index-wide enrichment, use token/phrase-aware product-type validation.","Tests largely assert generated strings on synthetic products. The only ranking test uses the exact injected word `подземное`; it does not establish ordinary-versus-specialised separation against realistic morphology, conflicts, negation, or source catalog records. Thus it insufficiently tests the hypothesis and safety claim."],"fix_plan":["Make descriptor detection phrase/token-aware and explicitly exclude negated regulating evidence before assigning the regulating descriptor; tighten the ball-valve type predicate.","Add focused tests for negated regulating language, near-miss/non-ball-valve text, and conflicting descriptor evidence.","Add retrieval tests with realistic tender forms (including ordinary versus specialised competing products) that prove the enrichment changes ranking in the intended direction while preserving nonmatching variants; then rerun the stated focused suite and public evaluation."],"next_direction":"Catalog-side enrichment remains a credible direction, but treat generated descriptors as normalized facts: require conservative evidence rules and demonstrate end-to-end candidate separation rather than only descriptor presence."}
